<h1>{{PROJECT_NAME}} — features map</h1>

<p>Read this before touching code: find the area, then open only the one or two feature pages it points at.</p>

<p>Read order: <code>HANDOFF.md</code> (current state) → this index → <strong>the page for your area</strong> → the code. <code>docs/SPEC.md</code> is user-visible behavior; <code>AGENTS.md</code> is process and hard rules; the pages below are the map, the mechanism and the traps.</p>

<p><strong>Keep this map true in the same commit as the change.</strong> Before staging a commit, update the feature page(s) for whatever you changed, and add a row here if the page is new. User-visible behavior also updates <code>docs/SPEC.md</code> in the same commit; a docs-only follow-up commit is the one that gets skipped. Pure refactors with no behavior, mechanism, config or test change need no edit.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Doc</th><th>Read when</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr data-placeholder="row"><td><!-- Feature name --></td><td><!-- page.html --></td><td><!-- the trigger phrase someone would search for: the file, the symbol, the symptom --></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<!-- One row per feature page. Keep "Read when" a trigger, not a restatement of
the title: it is the line an agent searches for when it does not yet know which
page it needs. The plugin fills this marked row from the pages when it
scaffolds or adopts a project, and rewrites it only when asked. -->

<p>The index is the entry point, not a glob: pages may live in subdirectories as well as at the top level, so <code>docs/features/*.html</code> alone misses them. Follow the row's link.</p>

<h2>Page format</h2>

<p>A feature page is an HTML document with an <code>h1</code> title, a <code>p class="status"</code> line and one <code>section</code> per heading. The headings are the retrieval unit: the tool returns one section at a time, so a section is what a later session reads on its own.</p>

<pre class="verbatim" data-why="the headings and the status line are the retrieval contract; copy them byte for byte">&lt;h1&gt;&lt;Feature&gt; — &lt;one-line summary&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p class="status"&gt;Status: shipped (vX.Y) · Read when: &lt;trigger&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;section id="what-it-does"&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;What it does&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;3–6 bullets; link &lt;code&gt;docs/SPEC.md&lt;/code&gt; for the user-visible detail.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;

&lt;section id="map"&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;Map&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Files, types, functions and test names — the anti-hunting payload. Names, never line numbers (they rot).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;

&lt;section id="how-why"&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;How &amp; why&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;The mechanism, the invariants and the traps: cause and effect, not a retelling of the bug that produced the rule.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;

&lt;section id="config-keys-ui"&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;Config keys / UI&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;table&gt;…&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;

&lt;section id="tests-that-pin-it"&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;Tests that pin it&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Test names — run these first when touching the area.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;

&lt;section id="related"&gt;
  &lt;h2&gt;Related&lt;/h2&gt;
  &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Links to the other feature pages.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;</pre>

<p>A code-shaped block is pseudocode and the default: <code>&lt;pre class="pseudo"&gt;</code>. Use <code>&lt;pre class="verbatim" data-why="…"&gt;</code> only when the exact bytes must survive, and say why — a verbatim block with no reason is how a copied block escapes review. A graph is <code>&lt;pre class="mermaid"&gt;</code>: the browser draws it and the tool hands an agent the edge list.</p>

<pre class="pseudo">probe fails → backoff doubles → retry after ceiling → surface the error</pre>

<p>Keep a page 60–200 lines and split past ~250, and keep a section under about two kilobytes: a section is what a later reader asks for by name, and one that outgrew a single read has to be split with <code>h3</code> before it can be handed over whole. No sentence lives in two docs: <code>docs/SPEC.md</code> is behavior, a feature page is map/mechanism/traps, <code>AGENTS.md</code> is process.</p>
